# tcpdrill, the retransmission timer, and the fact that it now backs off.
#
# WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FILE from tcp.drill's x01/x02.
#
# Adding exponential backoff makes sockets LIVE LONGER, and tcpdrill's case_end()
# closes the socket without completing the exchange, so the FIN it sends is
# never acknowledged and is retransmitted for as long as the timer allows.  With
# a flat one-second timer and ten retries that was ten seconds of noise, which
# mostly fell between cases.  With 1-2-4-8-16-32-64 it is 127 seconds, and every
# later case sees the previous case's FIN arrive in the middle of its own
# expectation ("wanted PA, got FA" with a sequence number from another socket).
#
# That is a harness isolation problem rather than a stack defect, and it is not
# fixed here.  What is done here instead is to make every case END WITH A RESET
# from the peer, which tears the socket down immediately in any state and leaves
# nothing retransmitting, so these cases measure the timer without depending on
# how the previous one finished.
#
# ALL TIMINGS ARE RELATIVE TO THE PREVIOUS MATCHED FRAME, from the E-Clock stamp
# the tap device takes inside BeginIO.  The bounds are wide (roughly +/- 30%)
# because the timer runs off the 50 Hz IP tick and the guest is a 14 MHz 68020:
# what is being asserted is DOUBLING, not a stopwatch reading.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

# RFC 6298 5.5.  The connection request is the cheapest place to watch it,
# because nothing has to be sent or acknowledged first.
#
# Before NX_TCP_RETRY_SHIFT was set, docs/RESEARCH.md 27 measured this sequence
# as 890 ms then 1002 ms, flat, forever, for ten retries.  What it must be now
# is 1, 2, 4 and 8 seconds.
case rt01_syn_retransmission_doubles
socket
connect
tx S seq=0 within=200
tx S seq=0 after=700  within=1400
tx S seq=0 after=1400 within=2800
tx S seq=0 after=2800 within=5600
tx S seq=0 after=5600 within=11000
rx R seq=0

# The same rule on data.  Nothing acknowledges the segment, so every one of
# these is a retransmission of the first, and the ladder is rt01's ladder.
#
# NO TAIL LOSS PROBE HERE, and that is the assertion, not an omission.  RFC
# 8985 7.2's PTO is a multiple of SRTT, and SRTT is ZERO on the first segment
# of a connection: the estimator is armed by the data send path, not by the
# handshake, so nothing has been measured yet.  This file used to carry a
# fifth line for a probe at 240 ms, which was the tick a sample would have
# been floored at standing in for the multiple -- and a probe is a
# retransmission, so Karn's algorithm abandoned the sample that was in flight.
# On any path slower than 240 ms that made the first sample unobtainable for
# the life of the connection.  An unmeasured connection is not probed at all
# now; rt05 is where a measured one is.
#
# Measured on a 14 MHz 68020: 994, 2003, 4009 and 8016 ms.  The bounds are
# rt01's, roughly +/- 30%, because what is asserted is the doubling.
case rt02_data_retransmission_doubles
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
send 100
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 within=300
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 after=700  within=1400
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 after=1400 within=2800
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 after=2800 within=5600
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 after=5600 within=11000
rx R seq=1

# And the interval is not merely growing, it is growing from the right place:
# the FIRST interval is still one second.  A backoff that started at four would
# also double and would be wrong.
case rt03_first_interval_is_one_second
socket
connect
tx S seq=0 within=200
tx S seq=0 after=800 within=1300
rx R seq=0

# AND THE TIMER IS NOT THE ONLY THING THAT RECOVERS A SEGMENT.
#
# RFC 5827 section 2 lowers the fast retransmit threshold to one less than the
# number of outstanding segments while there are fewer than four of them,
# because three duplicate acknowledgments are three segments arriving past the
# hole and a flight of two cannot produce them.  Two segments outstanding
# therefore retransmit on the FIRST duplicate acknowledgment, in the round it
# arrives in, not a second later.
#
# It is asserted here rather than in sack.drill because it has nothing to do
# with SACK: the acknowledgment below carries no blocks.  sack.drill s14 used
# to reach it by accident, with a two-segment flight and a comment describing
# a three-segment one, and could say nothing about when the retransmission
# happened -- it arrived between two of the injections it was counting.
case rt04_two_segments_retransmit_on_one_dupack
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=100
tx A seq=1 ack=1
send 100
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100
send 100
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100
rx A seq=1 ack=1 win=8192
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 within=400
notx 600
rx R seq=1

# THE TAIL LOSS PROBE, on a connection that has been measured.
#
# rt02 says an unmeasured connection is not probed.  This says what happens
# once there is a sample: `idle 200` between the segment leaving and the
# acknowledgment being injected is the round trip the stack sees, because the
# peer is inside the guest and there is no other delay in the loop.
#
# A first sample of R gives SRTT = R, so RFC 8985 7.2's PTO is 2R +
# NX_TCP_LOSS_PROBE_DELACK_MS, about 600 ms, while RFC 6298 2.2's timeout is
# 3R, well under NX_TCP_RTO_MINIMUM_MS and therefore one second.  The probe
# lands between them.  It leaves the retry count and the timeout it found, so
# the rung after it expires when it always would have, measured from the
# SEGMENT and not from the probe.
case rt05_a_measured_connection_gets_its_tail_loss_probe
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
send 100
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 within=300
idle 200
rx A seq=1 ack=101 win=8192
send 100
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 within=300
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 after=300 within=1000
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 after=150 within=900
rx R seq=101

# AND IT PROBES THE TAIL.
#
# RFC 8985 7.3 asks for the LAST unacknowledged segment.  The probe used to go
# through the ordinary retransmit path, which walks from the head of the
# transmit queue, so on a flight of more than one segment it resent the OLDEST
# -- the one three duplicate acknowledgments and the timeout both already
# recover -- and left the tail, which neither can reach and which is the whole
# reason 7.2 exists.  On a flight of one they are the same packet, which is
# why every case above this one would pass either way.
#
# So: two segments outstanding, and the probe has to carry seq=201.  An MSS of
# 100 and one full acknowledgment put the congestion window at exactly two
# segments, which is what lets both sends leave.
case rt06_the_probe_resends_the_tail_not_the_head
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=100
tx A seq=1 ack=1
send 100
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 within=300
idle 200
rx A seq=1 ack=101 win=8192
send 100
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 within=300
send 100
tx PA seq=201 ack=1 len=100 within=300
tx PA seq=201 ack=1 len=100 after=300 within=1000
rx R seq=201
